### What is the danger of reading large files the wrong way?

If you call `Files.readAllLines(path)` or `Files.readString(path)`, the entire file is loaded into memory. For a multi-gigabyte transaction log, this guarantees an `OutOfMemoryError`. Every good approach keeps only a bounded amount of data in memory at any instant.

```java
package com.bank.io;

import java.nio.file.Files;
import java.nio.file.Path;

public class WrongWay {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        // WRONG: loads entire file into memory
        // For a 10 GB file, this throws OutOfMemoryError
        var lines = Files.readAllLines(Path.of("transactions.csv"));
        System.out.println("Lines: " + lines.size());
    }
}
```

### How does BufferedReader.readLine() work?

`BufferedReader` wraps a `Reader` with an internal character buffer, typically 8192 characters. It reads a block from the underlying stream into the buffer and then hands you one line at a time via `readLine()`. Only the current line and the internal buffer are in memory, so the footprint is constant regardless of file size.

```java
package com.bank.io;

import java.io.BufferedReader;
import java.io.FileReader;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;

public class BufferedReaderExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        BigDecimal total = BigDecimal.ZERO;
        long count = 0;

        try (BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(
                new FileReader("transactions.csv", StandardCharsets.UTF_8))) {
            String line;
            while ((line = br.readLine()) != null) {
                // Each line: accountId,amount
                String[] parts = line.split(",");
                BigDecimal amount = new BigDecimal(parts[1]);
                total = total.add(amount);
                count++;
            }
        }
        System.out.println("Processed " + count + " transactions.");
        System.out.println("Total: " + total);
    }
}
```

```text
Processed 50000000 transactions.
Total: 1250000000000.00
```

### How does Files.lines() work and why must it be closed?

`Files.lines(path)` returns a lazy `Stream<String>` that reads lines on demand as the stream is consumed. It combines the bounded memory of `readLine()` with the expressiveness of the Streams API.

The critical caveat is that this stream holds an open file handle. It must be closed, which means using it inside a try-with-resources block. Forgetting to close it leaks handles, and under load you exhaust the OS handle limit.

```java
package com.bank.io;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.nio.file.Files;
import java.nio.file.Path;

public class FilesLinesExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        // CORRECT: try-with-resources closes the stream
        try (var lines = Files.lines(Path.of("transactions.csv"))) {
            BigDecimal total = lines
                .map(line -> line.split(",")[1])
                .map(BigDecimal::new)
                .reduce(BigDecimal.ZERO, BigDecimal::add);

            System.out.println("Total: " + total);
        }
    }
}
```

```java
// WRONG: stream never closed, leaks file handle
var total = Files.lines(Path.of("transactions.csv"))
    .map(line -> line.split(",")[1])
    .map(BigDecimal::new)
    .reduce(BigDecimal.ZERO, BigDecimal::add);
```

### Why is Scanner slow for large files?

`Scanner` synchronizes internally, compiles and applies regular expressions for delimiters, and does extra parsing per token. For small inputs this overhead is negligible, but for millions of lines you feel it sharply.

```java
package com.bank.io;

import java.io.File;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.Scanner;

public class ScannerExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        BigDecimal total = BigDecimal.ZERO;
        long count = 0;

        // Scanner works but is measurably slower than BufferedReader
        try (Scanner sc = new Scanner(new File("transactions.csv"))) {
            while (sc.hasNextLine()) {
                String line = sc.nextLine();
                String[] parts = line.split(",");
                total = total.add(new BigDecimal(parts[1]));
                count++;
            }
        }
        System.out.println("Scanner processed: " + count);
    }
}
```

### How does FileChannel with ByteBuffer give maximum throughput?

`FileChannel.read(ByteBuffer)` reads raw bytes into a reusable buffer. You decode those bytes with a `CharsetDecoder` and split on line boundaries yourself. This avoids the overhead of line-by-line string creation when you only need to extract numeric fields.

```java
package com.bank.io;

import java.io.RandomAccessFile;
import java.nio.ByteBuffer;
import java.nio.channels.FileChannel;
import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;

public class ChannelReader {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        try (RandomAccessFile raf = new RandomAccessFile("transactions.csv", "r");
             FileChannel channel = raf.getChannel()) {

            ByteBuffer buffer = ByteBuffer.allocateDirect(64 * 1024);
            long byteCount = 0;

            while (channel.read(buffer) > 0) {
                buffer.flip();
                byteCount += buffer.remaining();
                // Process raw bytes: decode, find newlines, extract fields
                buffer.clear();
            }
            System.out.println("Read " + byteCount + " bytes via channel.");
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Read 2147483648 bytes via channel.
```

### How do the approaches compare?

| Approach | Memory | Speed | Ease of use | Streaming? |
|----------|--------|-------|-------------|------------|
| `Files.readAllLines()` | Entire file in heap | Fast for small files | Very easy | No |
| `Scanner` | Bounded | Slow (regex overhead) | Easy | Yes |
| `BufferedReader.readLine()` | Bounded (buffer + line) | Fast | Easy | Yes |
| `Files.lines()` | Bounded (lazy stream) | Fast | Moderate (must close) | Yes |
| `FileChannel` + `ByteBuffer` | Bounded (buffer) | Fastest | Hard (manual decoding) | Yes |
| `MappedByteBuffer` | OS-managed pages | Fastest (zero-copy) | Hard (manual decoding) | Yes |

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is collecting the entire stream into a list, which defeats the purpose of streaming.

```java
// WRONG: collect(toList()) loads everything into memory
List<String> all = Files.lines(path).collect(Collectors.toList());

// CORRECT: reduce to a bounded result
BigDecimal total = Files.lines(path)
    .map(line -> new BigDecimal(line.split(",")[1]))
    .reduce(BigDecimal.ZERO, BigDecimal::add);
```

Another mistake is not specifying the charset, which causes data corruption across platforms.

```java
// WRONG: uses platform default charset
new BufferedReader(new FileReader("transactions.csv"));

// CORRECT: explicit charset
new BufferedReader(new FileReader("transactions.csv", StandardCharsets.UTF_8));
```

### What about parallel processing of large files?

For CPU-heavy per-line work, you can parallelize, but `Files.lines().parallel()` splits poorly on unindexed text. A better pattern is to read sequentially and dispatch line batches to a thread pool.

```java
package com.bank.io;

import java.io.BufferedReader;
import java.io.FileReader;
import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
import java.util.concurrent.Future;

public class BatchParallel {
    private static final int BATCH_SIZE = 10_000;

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(4);
        List<Future<?>> futures = new ArrayList<>();

        try (BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(
                new FileReader("transactions.csv", StandardCharsets.UTF_8))) {
            List<String> batch = new ArrayList<>(BATCH_SIZE);
            String line;
            while ((line = br.readLine()) != null) {
                batch.add(line);
                if (batch.size() == BATCH_SIZE) {
                    List<String> toProcess = new ArrayList<>(batch);
                    futures.add(pool.submit(() -> processBatch(toProcess)));
                    batch.clear();
                }
            }
            if (!batch.isEmpty()) {
                futures.add(pool.submit(() -> processBatch(batch)));
            }
        }
        for (Future<?> f : futures) f.get();
        pool.shutdown();
    }

    static void processBatch(List<String> lines) {
        // Heavy per-line processing here
        System.out.println("Processed batch of " + lines.size());
    }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Always specify the charset explicitly as `StandardCharsets.UTF_8`. For fixed-width or binary records, channels and mapping shine because you can address records by offset. For append-and-tail scenarios like watching a growing log, use a `FileChannel` with positional reads. And remember that the API choice is a performance decision, but streaming line-by-line is a correctness requirement.
